{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-8,-15).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-8506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-15)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{289} \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-15}{-8})| \\\\  & ≈1.080\\,84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.080\\,84 \\\\  & ≈4.22,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(17,4.22).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-8506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "362b8d76679c3eca283cd1ce145a45cf2eae7f3c9a5965e2bc12849f604d6b6f"} {"question" "q-8506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-8506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(15,4.22)"] ["math" "(15,4.83)"] ["math" "(17,1.08)"] ["math" "(17,4.22)"] ["math" "(17,4.83)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-8506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(17,4.22)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(5,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(5,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "362b8d76679c3eca283cd1ce145a45cf2eae7f3c9a5965e2bc12849f604d6b6f"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(25,0)"] ["math" "(25,\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(5,0)"] ["math" "(5,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-16947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5,0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,12).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "362b8d76679c3eca283cd1ce145a45cf2eae7f3c9a5965e2bc12849f604d6b6f"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(12,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(12,0)"] ["math" "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(12,\\pi )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-k,k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "362b8d76679c3eca283cd1ce145a45cf2eae7f3c9a5965e2bc12849f604d6b6f"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2\\sqrt{k},-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(2\\sqrt{k},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-40761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "362b8d76679c3eca283cd1ce145a45cf2eae7f3c9a5965e2bc12849f604d6b6f"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"} {"question" "q-275806" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011485/state.json" "file_sha256" "55befc77b0958848a94a41de8d66e2a0f51998108d39683c8137f414a16d4792"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1152 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
